WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.919 --> 00:00:07.340
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.724 --> 00:00:18.143
Ammonites first appeared on Earth

00:00:18.143 --> 00:00:23.273
around 400 million years ago, long before dinosaurs

00:00:23.273 --> 00:00:24.190
walked the Earth.

00:00:30.822 --> 00:00:35.285
They were extraordinarily abundant,

00:00:35.285 --> 00:00:39.664
with over 30,000 different species

00:00:39.664 --> 00:00:41.166
of all shapes and sizes.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:49.799
But what do we really know about the way they lived?

00:00:49.799 --> 00:00:52.051
In terms of lifestyles of ammonites,

00:00:52.051 --> 00:00:57.348
their morphology is so diverse that their range of lifestyles

00:00:57.348 --> 00:00:59.726
would have been really diverse.

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:03.354
Today, millions of fossilized ammonite shells

00:01:03.354 --> 00:01:06.858
are found all over the world.

00:01:06.858 --> 00:01:10.320
But they tell us frustratingly little about the animal that

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.864
lived inside.

00:01:12.864 --> 00:01:14.949
The greatest problem that we have is,

00:01:14.949 --> 00:01:17.076
there are only a small number of fossils

00:01:17.076 --> 00:01:20.079
that do actually preserve the soft parts of ammonites.

00:01:20.079 --> 00:01:23.082
And they never really give us the exact information

00:01:23.082 --> 00:01:25.752
that we would like.

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:31.424
So how do we know anything about these mysterious animals?

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:33.593
We're quite lucky, in that ammonites

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:35.512
belong to the large group of mollusks

00:01:35.512 --> 00:01:36.930
called the cephalopods.

00:01:39.474 --> 00:01:42.477
These include the modern day Nautilus,

00:01:42.477 --> 00:01:46.814
and it provides crucial evidence about what ammonites were like.

00:01:49.734 --> 00:01:52.987
Nautilus today has a chambered shell,

00:01:52.987 --> 00:01:56.741
and the animal itself lives in the very last chamber.

00:01:56.741 --> 00:02:02.413
The morphology of ammonite shells is almost identical.

00:02:02.413 --> 00:02:05.166
We now believe, like Nautilus, ammonites

00:02:05.166 --> 00:02:08.962
had tentacles, well-developed eyes,

00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:11.589
and used the chambers inside their shells

00:02:11.589 --> 00:02:15.593
for stability in the water.

00:02:15.593 --> 00:02:17.679
Nautiloids today, as ammonites would have done,

00:02:17.679 --> 00:02:20.556
control their buoyancy, just like a submarine,

00:02:20.556 --> 00:02:25.186
by emptying the chambers of water to be able to float

00:02:25.186 --> 00:02:27.313
and refilling them to be able to sink.

00:02:30.775 --> 00:02:36.948
A different modern cephalopod shows us how ammonites swam.

00:02:36.948 --> 00:02:40.326
An octopus propels itself by squirting water

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:42.328
through a tube called a siphon.

00:02:45.248 --> 00:02:48.251
All cephalopods have siphons, and it's

00:02:48.251 --> 00:02:53.923
likely some ammonites used theirs to swim at great speed.

00:02:53.923 --> 00:02:56.050
Some of them with fast moving predators,

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:58.094
adapted for fairly rapid movement.

00:02:58.094 --> 00:03:01.639
But others appear to have been slow swimmers, feeding

00:03:01.639 --> 00:03:02.390
on plankton.

00:03:07.729 --> 00:03:10.857
Ammonites were extraordinarily, successful animals

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:15.194
they existed twice as long as the dinosaurs.

00:03:15.194 --> 00:03:16.946
So why did they go extinct?

00:03:22.118 --> 00:03:24.954
Most ammonites went extinct about 66 million years ago,

00:03:24.954 --> 00:03:30.209
about the same time as the dinosaurs went extinct.

00:03:30.209 --> 00:03:34.046
An asteroid six miles wide struck the Earth,

00:03:34.046 --> 00:03:37.800
causing major impacts to many ecosystems,

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.803
including the shallow seas.

00:03:40.803 --> 00:03:44.223
Ammonites did have one critical weakness

00:03:44.223 --> 00:03:47.393
compared to other cephalopods.

00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:49.687
They appear to have been constrained

00:03:49.687 --> 00:03:53.816
to laying their eggs in fairly shallow water.

00:03:53.816 --> 00:03:57.653
This put the young at the mercy of the catastrophic changes

00:03:57.653 --> 00:04:02.408
to the food chain after the asteroid impact.

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:03.785
Photosynthesis wouldn't have been

00:04:03.785 --> 00:04:05.703
able to occur so much in the planktonic plant

00:04:05.703 --> 00:04:06.662
life in the oceans.

00:04:06.662 --> 00:04:08.039
And that would have had a greater

00:04:08.039 --> 00:04:09.624
impact on the shallow oceans.

00:04:09.624 --> 00:04:11.083
And the shallow oceans were where

00:04:11.083 --> 00:04:12.168
the ammonites were living.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:19.050
It spelled the end to 300 million years

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:22.178
of ammonite evolution.

00:04:22.178 --> 00:04:27.058
But they left behind a fascinating legacy

00:04:27.058 --> 00:04:32.730
and a crucial insight into life in the prehistoric scenes.

